#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "426f5205-e4dc-5e33-b797-1f55019e14d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288483} {:answer/id #uuid "988141a9-9be5-59b2-8abd-837c9c5652aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289054} {:answer/id #uuid "cd825353-57a8-52b4-9d0b-ae4303b47a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289403} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e9d989-c2e7-5e61-85c6-2c964c681758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289670} {:answer/id #uuid "f9628a6b-8330-5dfc-b634-fd26bd7aa9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "988141a9-9be5-59b2-8abd-837c9c5652aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289054} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8b257f8-6e6b-5962-b0e6-3ea368c98ae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f7deccd-b00e-58b4-a06a-44a78bd3e26d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20988" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b77bdde611689095cdea6a3a08ce9828b9e20040283dc0c251250538a5cfba3.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,y={t}^{2}-1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6t-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t}{6t-2}=\\frac{t}{3t-1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20fed31d-5bfd-56fb-b736-1523716f3623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288242} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a45a52-bb1d-5298-8eea-b0736136a858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289203} {:answer/id #uuid "b35e4f36-00ed-5382-b39e-4e0a8616fde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289215} {:answer/id #uuid "d48c86fe-0944-5271-85f8-d01acc9ec550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289442} {:answer/id #uuid "df29cd35-a94b-54f6-a005-8167cc54c05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b35e4f36-00ed-5382-b39e-4e0a8616fde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289215} :answer-field/id #uuid "edae3f4a-489e-5181-a3c8-af691c065008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14df7ed3-c243-590c-b10b-e1904947b459" :question/math-academy-id "q-21072" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a02222e-7bac-556f-8a59-fb96cc7379fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288430} {:answer/id #uuid "54e72178-1f7b-5214-9ba6-6c902e98a7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288618} {:answer/id #uuid "86ae557a-8091-5236-b106-cb6c715bfdf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288940} {:answer/id #uuid "870e697f-06aa-529c-aeec-3d2b85541b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288942} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1d45d8-de11-5e5a-b944-94183a2a9ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54e72178-1f7b-5214-9ba6-6c902e98a7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288618} :answer-field/id #uuid "80f53a4c-1f61-543b-a7a8-834f7fc0d410" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92fe000e-8b10-5f6c-a7a8-e9bb8ce2fa57" :question/math-academy-id "q-24378" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =1-2{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ea4864c-b306-57e1-b44c-21b26487fe84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288575} {:answer/id #uuid "99103521-803c-5804-a165-119989cc93da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289057} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f66a3c-9003-56a0-98dc-04353efeac62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289315} {:answer/id #uuid "c824007d-3cd4-55bb-995f-0ebfb53b60b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{x}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289358} {:answer/id #uuid "e69d7548-01ac-54b0-b01f-7d230602a3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ea4864c-b306-57e1-b44c-21b26487fe84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288575} :answer-field/id #uuid "0da5f05f-ddec-577d-bb96-889893b10755" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50632a56-05a3-5b2c-a470-4387daec2008" :question/math-academy-id "q-24574" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}\\right)-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1 \\\\ y & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c635f8-a836-5e71-b883-8a1bd5cf25e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288196} {:answer/id #uuid "2bacbcf5-685c-51b1-88e0-85a788eecc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288318} {:answer/id #uuid "87cc2f4e-738c-52b7-abe2-f860d6aed289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288948} {:answer/id #uuid "9086facf-67d8-55b3-ba11-e7e20d058362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288999} {:answer/id #uuid "d34b06b8-fbc5-51ef-94d2-add58e6f047b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d34b06b8-fbc5-51ef-94d2-add58e6f047b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289432} :answer-field/id #uuid "468c38a2-26d6-518c-b2b0-6ef0ade60949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca1f80dd-221f-5c68-b74a-e39a400a3763" :question/math-academy-id "q-24575" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =2{\\left(-\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1 \\\\ y & =4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54369419-cb7a-53a4-ad10-1cef5f94f9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288612} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3315c5-e581-5704-8feb-0fa7e40d938b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288885} {:answer/id #uuid "af7ea6ab-b4ce-5db0-93b2-7941e17fe43e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289193} {:answer/id #uuid "e910a38f-f4e4-5977-9d9f-7ed4f36b6aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-18+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289579} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b76b41-917c-54ab-ab82-87ce9743027a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54369419-cb7a-53a4-ad10-1cef5f94f9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288612} :answer-field/id #uuid "85d607bd-1288-57e3-afbf-e5c537389a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd03cb1f-af7d-59db-bf84-fa1a13cb0a30" :question/math-academy-id "q-24576" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2{\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}\\right) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ y & =-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6aa2428-7dcb-5d6c-a5fe-f375a8babfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6aa2428-7dcb-5d6c-a5fe-f375a8babfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291360} :answer-field/id #uuid "11307bb1-92af-5545-b56b-9fbb89a8c09c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a2d7f5-349e-51c5-97ac-ff980b128414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13a2d7f5-349e-51c5-97ac-ff980b128414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290714} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b8750d5-781f-572d-950a-89954f43502e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd983c35-bcbe-51c4-9f91-e3002055ca8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-275161" :question/problem "![](images/05/05a4ea8872b0e1197a248f412b4d1e51a170cc04b2da24aa7f6d05679985a74e.png)\n\nThe arc of the circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/3f/3f259a44786f9851588e212396f1c9fe3259a63c88b8c3090ebc6f1e50a6c456.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb08236c-022a-5747-8dc6-3e7c0d10e3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb08236c-022a-5747-8dc6-3e7c0d10e3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291616} :answer-field/id #uuid "135bf121-2964-517c-bc51-bd47c506306d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291802} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa8af0b0-a318-5f98-9796-25f5b81c8cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa8af0b0-a318-5f98-9796-25f5b81c8cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291375} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e7ac31-018a-5f68-ada0-43030f3ea0c8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292006} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32492bcd-4183-5a95-ae5f-9f5355868077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32492bcd-4183-5a95-ae5f-9f5355868077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290835} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfdd6437-8c69-50eb-a5ab-bb20130eeec8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47f34483-7692-5e5a-8304-62b8ecc1d561" :question/math-academy-id "q-275275" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨6,-2,0⟩$ and $v=⟨5,0,-3⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot 0)i-(6\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot 5)j+(6\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot 5)k \\\\  & =(6-0)i-(-18-0)j+(0-(-10))k \\\\  & =6i+18j+10k \\\\  & =⟨6,18,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b70cc5e3-ce9b-587a-856e-446f9654bd7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b70cc5e3-ce9b-587a-856e-446f9654bd7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291425} :answer-field/id #uuid "088b5a0c-ad44-5fc4-9428-330d1ccbe936" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5deb2570-5a15-57c0-8f85-5aad6b606da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5deb2570-5a15-57c0-8f85-5aad6b606da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291033} :answer-field/id #uuid "18dbf2c9-5d0b-5815-b5f5-495a6394fcc0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c72f4aa-ab5b-5b58-b34b-802301025714" :question/math-academy-id "q-275278" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-2x,3,5⟩,$ $b=⟨2x,0,1⟩$ and $|\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{909}.$ Then, in **ascending order**,  the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2x & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2x & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2x & 5 \\\\ 2x & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2x & 3 \\\\ 2x & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3-0)i-(-2x-10x)j+(0-6x)k \\\\  & =⟨3,12x,-6x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(12x)^{2}+(-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+144{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{180{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{909} \\\\ \\sqrt{180{x}^{2}+9} & =\\sqrt{909} \\\\ 180{x}^{2}+9 & =909 \\\\ 180{x}^{2} & =900 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\sqrt{5},$ $x=\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a911054-c5bb-5029-8afa-8d953ec079d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a911054-c5bb-5029-8afa-8d953ec079d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290743} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff116d0-793f-5dec-9b7a-1f999289cb92" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291853} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fa8b2ec-7940-569c-bacf-94019cc63207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fa8b2ec-7940-569c-bacf-94019cc63207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290972} :answer-field/id #uuid "485d6ab9-cde1-5618-b061-0978e3c079c7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291967} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbc666df-928f-50c4-b815-3ae4f2924c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbc666df-928f-50c4-b815-3ae4f2924c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291702} :answer-field/id #uuid "b20085d3-a7a3-553b-a6da-d04d5f933768" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "52360184-f3de-54ee-a0c9-a89eddeb575c" :question/math-academy-id "q-275313" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=36.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e75192d-d55e-5612-87e9-5c19a9bc0579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e75192d-d55e-5612-87e9-5c19a9bc0579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290700} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b4447ef-bb30-5505-b1c3-d8bc339f5382" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72cfd9b9-a8b6-5fcc-92da-c6a5b78e755e" :question/math-academy-id "q-275347" :question/problem "Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ given that $a=⟨-\\sqrt{3},0,-2⟩$ and $b\\times c=⟨-\\sqrt{3},10,-10⟩.$\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-\\sqrt{3},0,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},10,-10⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{3})\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3})+0\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =3+0+20 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0e5a73a-2919-5f8c-a396-7d6e2d9fe189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0e5a73a-2919-5f8c-a396-7d6e2d9fe189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291402} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcec4611-b8bf-5bde-af83-ce1e61831acc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae335859-0dfa-58d7-a7ca-cf642f79ca2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-275348" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨1,2,3⟩,$ $b=⟨-1,0,-2⟩,$ and $c=⟨0,-2,1⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2))i-((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-2))j+((-1)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-4i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-4,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4+2+6 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c1e3733-5463-5a73-87fe-9bad262f9b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c1e3733-5463-5a73-87fe-9bad262f9b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291231} :answer-field/id #uuid "60cd938e-fe0c-506b-935f-e549ff5c73c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d53568b9-73ac-5ba0-8169-82ca6f9fc7df" :question/math-academy-id "q-275595" :question/problem "Evaluate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & y \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & y \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}-y\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "637424aa-e375-5ca5-bc92-9b93538da967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "637424aa-e375-5ca5-bc92-9b93538da967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291058} :answer-field/id #uuid "5deef2d0-b2e7-54ab-a3df-7734195f5705" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d712990-6aa9-538b-af72-704fc59cbac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-275598" :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(C)=-14,$ determine the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & k \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 6-k\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6-4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(C)=-14$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =-14 \\\\ 6-4k & =-14 \\\\ -4k & =-14-6 \\\\ -4k & =-20 \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93399e4d-4d85-580e-9e08-c041fa5d5800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93399e4d-4d85-580e-9e08-c041fa5d5800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291266} :answer-field/id #uuid "339de4a9-ce7b-517d-9496-4255c739b1c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d673c24b-03d4-55e8-a5fb-605bc1d6c21f" :question/math-academy-id "q-275601" :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3+p & 4p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=7,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3+p & 4p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3+p)(5-p)-(4p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =15-3p+5p-{p}^{2}-4p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =15-2p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =15-2p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $det(A)=7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =7 \\\\ 15-2p & =7 \\\\ -2p & =-8 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8605aaa0-1b98-56eb-a570-a43a2a70d923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8605aaa0-1b98-56eb-a570-a43a2a70d923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291204} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a6ce4b8-3146-5a15-914e-728e1ea47524" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291775} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "951222c4-e5b5-5571-9a2a-d0eb2efb77b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "951222c4-e5b5-5571-9a2a-d0eb2efb77b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291278} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bb5b0ed-47e4-542c-9624-f45a9ae12fb8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291974} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3904da6-dc56-5079-9dea-00671367b5a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3904da6-dc56-5079-9dea-00671367b5a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291471} :answer-field/id #uuid "69dce617-2d0a-5338-ac15-f289ffb850c5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202c5215-8f3f-596e-a440-8907945c8a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "202c5215-8f3f-596e-a440-8907945c8a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290768} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0b94bb1-0aab-5a9f-85cc-3defdfc1d265" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "507bf285-e98e-529f-bfee-b764a50e3854" :question/math-academy-id "q-276257" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5-4t,\\,y=t+1,\\,t\\ge -1.\n$$\n\nDetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $x=5-4t\\le 9$ for all $t\\ge -1.$ Consequently, the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\le 9.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that $y=t+1\\ge 0$ for all $t\\ge -1.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890558a3-9356-5d6e-aa4a-ad58edb49c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "890558a3-9356-5d6e-aa4a-ad58edb49c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291214} :answer-field/id #uuid "03bdef3a-470f-56e8-a9e2-85dfe88918ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21859e3d-c55d-5cf1-b718-09d763da06c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186